  "image_name": "1bc1eb35a286871afe7e2be214177004.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how altered fatty acid metabolism in a cancer cell promotes immune evasion in the tumor microenvironment. It highlights several linked routes: increased fatty acid oxidation in mitochondria, increased fatty acid synthesis, greater uptake of external fatty acids, and accumulation of intracellular lipids and lipid droplets.\n\nOn the left, mitochondrial fatty acid oxidation involving CPT1 is associated with higher MHC class I expression and higher CD47 expression, while ARF1 appears linked to reduced immunogenic stress and cell death. These changes affect nearby immune cells differently, with increased CTL activation but reduced phagocytosis by tumor-associated macrophages and lower dendritic cell activation.\n\nIn the center and right, enhanced FASN-driven synthesis and fatty acid transporter-mediated uptake enlarge the intracellular fatty acid pool and lipid droplets. In the tumor microenvironment, CD36-mediated fatty acid uptake is shown to suppress antitumor immunity: CTLs become less active, dendritic cells show poorer CTL priming, and regulatory T cells gain function. Lipid droplets and COX2 are also connected to increased eicosanoid synthesis, which further weakens CTL and NK cell killing, reduces dendritic cell activation, and increases macrophage-mediated immunosuppression.\n\nOverall, the image depicts lipid metabolism as a major driver of tumor immune suppression.",
